  "live_records": {
    "q-99409": {
      "dom_id": "step-q99409",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "Find the binormal vector to the curve $r(t)$ at an arbitrary point, given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t,0,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t⟩, \\\\ N(t) & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t,0,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $T$ and $N$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal, respectively.",
      "worked_solution": "The binormal vector is given by\n\n$$\nB(t)=T(t)\\times N(t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(t) & =T(t)\\times N(t) \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t,0,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t⟩\\times ⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t,0,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡3t & 0 & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t & 0 & -\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =[\\text{ }\\left|\\begin{aligned}0 & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t \\\\ 0 & -\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,i-\\left|\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t & -\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,j+\\left|\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t & 0 \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =⟨0-0,-(-{cos}^{2}⁡3t-{sin}^{2}⁡3t),0-0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,1,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
