    "q-127612": {
      "dom_id": "step-q127612",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "Find the binormal vector to the curve $r(t)$ at an arbitrary point, given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨\\sqrt{3},-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩, \\\\ N(t) & =⟨0,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $T$ and $N$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal, respectively.",
      "worked_solution": "The binormal vector is given by\n\n$$\nB(t)=T(t)\\times N(t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(t) & =T(t)\\times N(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨\\sqrt{3},-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\times ⟨0,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ \\sqrt{3} & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & \\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 0 & -\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\text{ }\\left|\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & \\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,i-\\left|\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3} & \\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 0 & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,j+\\left|\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3} & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 0 & -\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨({sin}^{2}⁡t-(-{cos}^{2}⁡t)),-(-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-0),(-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-0)⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨1,\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
