      "knowledge_point": "Calculating the General Binormal Vector to a Curve Given the Unit Tangent and Principal Normal",
      "kp_id": "e-8595"
    },
    "q-98807": {
      "dom_id": "step-q98807",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Find the binormal vector to the curve $r(t)$ at an arbitrary point, given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}⟨-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,1⟩, \\\\ N(t) & =⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $T$ and $N$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal, respectively.",
      "worked_solution": "The binormal vector is given by\n\n$$\nB(t)=T(t)\\times N(t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(t) & =T(t)\\times N(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}⟨-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,1⟩\\times ⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\,\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ -3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & 1 \\\\ -\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\text{ }\\left|\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & 1 \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,i-\\left|\\begin{aligned}-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & 1 \\\\ -\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,j+\\left|\\begin{aligned}-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}⟨(0+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),-(0+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),(3{sin}^{2}⁡t+3{cos}^{2}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
