    "q-127617": {
      "dom_id": "step-q127617",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "Find the binormal vector to the curve $r(t)$ at an arbitrary point, given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+4{t}^{2}}}⟨1,2t,0⟩, \\\\ N(t) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+4{t}^{2}}}⟨-2t,1,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $T$ and $N$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal, respectively.",
      "worked_solution": "The binormal vector is given by\n\n$$\nB(t)=T(t)\\times N(t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(t) & =T(t)\\times N(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+4{t}^{2}}}⟨1,2t,0⟩\\times \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+4{t}^{2}}}⟨-2t,1,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+4{t}^{2}}\\,\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 2t & 0 \\\\ -2t & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+4{t}^{2}}[\\text{ }\\left|\\begin{aligned}2t & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,i-\\left|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2t & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,j+\\left|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2t \\\\ -2t & 1\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+4{t}^{2}}⟨0-0,-(0-0),1+4{t}^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+4{t}^{2}}⟨0,0,1+4{t}^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,0,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
