    "q-127626": {
      "dom_id": "step-q127626",
      "name": "Question 8",
      "problem": "Find the binormal vector to the curve $r(t)$ at the point $t=2$, given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(2) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨0,1,2⟩, \\\\ N(2) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨0,2,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $T$ and $N$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal, respectively.",
      "worked_solution": "The binormal vector is given by\n\n$$\nB(t)=T(t)\\times N(t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(2) & =T(2)\\times N(2) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨0,1,2⟩\\times \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨0,2,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\text{ }\\left|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,i-\\left|\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,j+\\left|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨(-1-4),-(0-0),(0-0)⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨-5,0,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
