    "q-96959": {
      "dom_id": "step-q96959",
      "name": "Question 12",
      "problem": "Find the binormal vector to the curve $r(t)$ at an arbitrary point, given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨2\\sqrt{2},-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $T$ denotes the unit tangent vector.",
      "worked_solution": "The binormal vector is given by\n\n$$\nB(t)=T(t)\\times N(t),\n$$\n\nwhere the principal normal vector $N(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nN(t)=\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal normal $N(t).$ To do this, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and its magnitude as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\sqrt{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨0,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\frac{1}{3}\\sqrt{(0)^{2}+{(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}+{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we normalize ${T}^{′}(t)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(t) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}=\\frac{\\frac{1}{3}⟨0,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩}{(\\frac{1}{3})}=⟨0,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the binormal vector, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(t) & =T(t)\\times N(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨2\\sqrt{2},-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\times ⟨0,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2\\sqrt{2} & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & \\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 0 & -\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\text{ }\\left|\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & \\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,i-\\left|\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{2} & \\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 0 & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,j+\\left|\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{2} & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 0 & -\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨({sin}^{2}⁡t-(-{cos}^{2}⁡t)),-(-2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-0),(-2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-0)⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨1,\\,2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,-2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
