    "q-99583": {
      "dom_id": "step-q99583",
      "name": "Question 13",
      "problem": "Find the binormal vector to the curve $r(t)$ at an arbitrary point, given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5{e}^{2t}+1}}⟨2{e}^{t},{e}^{t},1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $T$ denotes the unit tangent vector.",
      "worked_solution": "The binormal vector is given by\n\n$$\nB(t)=T(t)\\times N(t),\n$$\n\nwhere the principal normal vector $N(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nN(t)=\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal normal $N(t).$ To do this, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and its magnitude as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{2{e}^{t}}{\\sqrt{5{e}^{2t}+1}}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{e}^{t}}{\\sqrt{5{e}^{2t}+1}}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5{e}^{2t}+1}})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{2{e}^{t}}{(5{e}^{2t}+1)^{3/2}},\\,\\frac{{e}^{t}}{(5{e}^{2t}+1)^{3/2}},\\,-\\frac{5{e}^{2t}}{(5{e}^{2t}+1)^{3/2}}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(5{e}^{2t}+1)^{3/2}}⟨2{e}^{t},\\,{e}^{t},\\,-5{e}^{2t}⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\frac{1}{(5{e}^{2t}+1)^{3/2}}\\sqrt{(2{e}^{t})^{2}+{({e}^{t})}^{2}+{(-5{e}^{2t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(5{e}^{2t}+1)^{3/2}}\\sqrt{4{e}^{2t}+{e}^{2t}+25{e}^{4t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}{e}^{t}}{(5{e}^{2t}+1)^{3/2}}\\sqrt{1+5{e}^{2t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}{e}^{t}}{5{e}^{2t}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we normalize ${T}^{′}(t)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(t) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}=\\frac{\\frac{1}{(5{e}^{2t}+1)^{3/2}}⟨2{e}^{t},\\,{e}^{t},\\,-5{e}^{2t}⟩}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}{e}^{t}}{5{e}^{2t}+1})}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}\\sqrt{5{e}^{2t}+1}}⟨2,\\,1,\\,-5{e}^{t}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the binormal vector, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(t) & =T(t)\\times N(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5{e}^{2t}+1}}⟨2{e}^{t},{e}^{t},1⟩\\times \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}\\sqrt{5{e}^{2t}+1}}⟨2,\\,1,\\,-5{e}^{t}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}(5{e}^{2t}+1)}\\,\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2{e}^{t} & {e}^{t} & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -5{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}(5{e}^{2t}+1)}[\\text{ }\\left|\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} & 1 \\\\ 1 & -5{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,i-\\left|\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{t} & 1 \\\\ 2 & -5{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,j+\\left|\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{t} & {e}^{t} \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}(5{e}^{2t}+1)}⟨-5{e}^{2t}-1,-(-10{e}^{2t}-2),2{e}^{t}-2{e}^{t}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}(5{e}^{2t}+1)}⟨-(5{e}^{2t}+1),\\,2(5{e}^{2t}+1),\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨-1,2,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
