    },
    "q-99652": {
      "dom_id": "step-q99652",
      "name": "Question 14",
      "problem": "Find the binormal vector to the curve $r(t)$ at an arbitrary point, given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1),2\\sqrt{2},\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1)⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $T$ denotes the unit tangent vector.",
      "worked_solution": "The binormal vector is given by\n\n$$\nB(t)=T(t)\\times N(t),\n$$\n\nwhere the principal normal vector $N(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nN(t)=\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal normal $N(t).$ To do this, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and its magnitude as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1)),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\sqrt{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1))⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1),\\,0,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1)⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\frac{1}{3}\\sqrt{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1))^{2}+{(0)}^{2}+{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1))}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡(t+1)+{sin}^{2}⁡(t+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we normalize ${T}^{′}(t)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(t) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}=\\frac{\\frac{1}{3}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1),\\,0,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1)⟩}{(\\frac{1}{3})}=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1),\\,0,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1)⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the binormal vector, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(t) & =T(t)\\times N(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1),2\\sqrt{2},\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1)⟩\\times ⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1),\\,0,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1)⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\,\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1) & 2\\sqrt{2} & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1) & 0 & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1)\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\text{ }\\left|\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{2} & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1) \\\\ 0 & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1)\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,i-\\left|\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1) & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1)\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,j+\\left|\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1) & 2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1) & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨-2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1)-0,-(-{sin}^{2}⁡(t+1)-{cos}^{2}⁡(t+1)),0-2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1)⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨-2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1),\\,1,\\,-2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1)⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
